      "dom_id": "question-112034",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the equation $6{w}^{2}+3w-5=2{w}^{2}-5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{w}^{2}+3w-5 & =2{w}^{2}-5 \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+3w-5-(2{w}^{2}-5) & =0 \\\\ 6{w}^{2}+3w-5-2{w}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (6{w}^{2}-2{w}^{2})+3w+(-5+5) & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+3w & =0 \\\\ w(4w+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nw=0\\,\\text{or}\\,4w+3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nw=0\\,\\text{or}\\,w=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=-\\frac{3}{4},0.$",
